Private libsamplerate 0.2.2 backend for AudioResampler
===================================================

Origin: https://github.com/libsndfile/libsamplerate/tree/0.2.2
Files retain upstream notices; COPYING is the upstream BSD-2-Clause licence.
The install rules ship COPYING under the product data directory, in
licenses/libsamplerate.
The coefficient tables and DSP arithmetic are unmodified. The system library
remains available to SampleBuffer, plug-ins and the independent reference tests.
CMake builds a static library; prefix.h gives its public API a private namespace.

Local change (cluster test-ControlExportSettings): src_sinc.c reserves its full
input-buffer capacity with malloc rather than calloc. sinc_reset invalidates the
buffer indices without clearing the unused capacity. prepare_data clears the
initial left history when the first input arrives and its length is known from
the ratio. All other readable samples are written by the existing input copy,
compaction or end padding. Neither reset nor process allocates memory. Maximum
ratio, buffer capacity, coefficient tables, selected quality and the engine's
pool capacity are unchanged. Used voices retain their own separate history.

Why: each SincBest stereo buffer reserves about 860 KiB for the worst supported
ratio. Clearing every unused slot in a roughly 15,000-slot pool made a quality
switch commit about 14 GiB of resident memory. A malloc reservation does not
write those unused pages. RSS now follows the input space actually written,
within the same fixed capacity. This does not promise that all voices running
at the worst ratio simultaneously fit below 6 GiB, or that first-touch page
faults have bounded latency. There are no malloc/free/VM calls on the processing
path; this is not an mlock/prefault guarantee.

AudioResamplerPoolMemoryTest checks all available pool slots in one burst,
including their first samples, and compares reset/streaming against the
unmodified system library across the three sinc qualities, five channel counts,
ratio changes and the ratio endpoints. The train tests also run under an
unchanged per-process resource cap.
